module Core

import Native

{-
   The host's own answer, which is what a JS build decides a float's digits with.

   **A ruling rather than a shortcut**, and the argument is that `Number.prototype.toExponential()`
   with no argument is *specified* to produce the shortest decimal that reads back as the number -
   which is the same sentence Ryu's correctness theorem is. Reading a float's bits at all on this
   target would mean the 64-bit `Double`/`I64` bitcast the language deliberately does not have, so
   the alternative is not a slower Ryu but no Ryu.

   A `.js.yana` file, so a native build never reads it - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5. It answers the
   same two names `Ryu.native.yana` does, and `Ryu.yana` holds the `Shortest` both produce.
-}

{-
   The JS half of the same two, which is the host's own answer read back out of a string.

   `toExponential()` with no argument is *specified* to be the shortest decimal that reads back as
   the number, so the double's digits arrive in one call. A `Float` is a `number` here as well, so
   the host's shortest is the shortest for the wrong type - `0.1` at single precision is a different
   number from `0.1` and has different digits - and what finds the right one is the round trip: the
   fewest digits whose value, narrowed back to `Float`, is the value that was asked about.

   Nine is where the walk stops because nine digits round-trip every `Float` there is, so the last
   step needs no test.
-}
fn doubleShortest(value: Double) -> Shortest =
    evenAtTie(value, shortestOfText(hostToExponential(value)))

fn floatShortest(value: Float) -> Shortest:
    let wide = value :: Double
    let &places = 0 :: Int

    while places < 8:
        let text = hostToExponentialAt(wide, places)
        if (truncate(hostToNumber(text)) :: Float) == value then return evenAtTie(wide, shortestOfText(text))
        places = places + 1

    return evenAtTie(wide, shortestOfText(hostToExponentialAt(wide, 8)))

{-
   Whether the value's decimal expansion stops after `count` significant digits.

   Every finite binary float has a *terminating* decimal expansion, so this is a real question with a
   yes or no answer rather than an approximation - and a hundred and one digits is where the host is
   asked for it, which is the widest `toExponential` takes. That width is exact for every value this
   is asked about: a tie needs the expansion to stop within nineteen digits, and where it does the
   remaining eighty-two are the value's own zeros rather than a rounding that produced some.

   Read off the text rather than through `shortestOfText`, because a hundred and one digits is four
   times what a `U64` holds and the question is only about which of them are zero.
-}
fn terminatesAt(value: Double, count: Int) -> Bool:
    let text = hostToExponentialAt(value, 100)
    let units = length(text)
    let &seen = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < units:
        let unit = stringUnit(text, i)
        if unit == 101 || unit == 69 then return True

        -- The sign and the point are not digits; everything else in a `toExponential` result is.
        if unit != 46 && unit != 45 && unit != 43:
            seen = seen + 1
            if seen > count && unit != 48 then return False

        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   The one case the host and Ryu disagree about, corrected so that the two targets never do.

   Both pick the digits closest to the value. Where two are *equally* close - where the exact value
   sits exactly on the midpoint between them - the specification says to take the larger and Ryu
   takes the even one, which is the same rule IEEE rounding itself uses and is the one worth keeping:
   ties-to-larger biases a whole column of a table upwards, and at single precision the two rules
   disagree often enough to show up in a few thousand random values.

   A tie is recognizable exactly, and it needs two things: the expansion one digit past the shortest
   form has to end in a five, and it has to *stop* there. The first is one call and rejects almost
   everything; the second is the expensive one and is only reached by a value that already looks like
   a tie. Both are needed - `2.0515849165496095e186` passes the first and fails the second, because
   what the host wrote at eighteen digits was a rounding that happened to land on a five.

   `digits - 1` cannot lose a digit or gain a trailing zero. It is only reached for an odd number, so
   it is never a power of ten; and a shortest form whose last digit is zero would have been one digit
   shorter, which is what `decimalLength` reading the *value* rather than a written width means.
-}
fn evenAtTie(value: Double, shortest: Shortest) -> Shortest:
    if shortest.digits % 2 == 0 then return shortest

    let count = decimalLength(shortest.digits)
    let probe = shortestOfText(hostToExponentialAt(value, count))

    if probe.exponent != shortest.exponent - 1 then return shortest
    if probe.digits != (shortest.digits - 1) * 10 + 5 then return shortest
    if !terminatesAt(value, count + 1) then return shortest

    return Shortest {digits: shortest.digits - 1, exponent: shortest.exponent}

{-
   `d.ddde±x`, read back into the pair the renderer wants.

   The host writes the point after the first digit and this wants the digits as one integer, so the
   exponent it reports is the written one less however many digits followed the point. Trailing
   zeros are removed afterwards, which the no-argument form never produces and the `n`-digit form
   always can: `decimalLength` counts what the number is written with, so a `1500` that means `15`
   would be rendered two digits too wide.
-}
fn shortestOfText(text: String) -> Shortest:
    let units = length(text)
    let &digits = 0 :: U64
    let &fractional = 0 :: Int
    let &afterPoint = False
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < units:
        let unit = stringUnit(text, i)
        if unit == 101 || unit == 69 then break

        if unit == 46:
            afterPoint = True
        else:
            digits = digits * 10 + wideCount(unit - 48)
            if afterPoint then fractional = fractional + 1

        i = i + 1

    let &exponent = 0 :: Int
    let &negative = False
    i = i + 1

    if i < units:
        let unit = stringUnit(text, i)
        if unit == 45:
            negative = True
            i = i + 1
        else if unit == 43:
            i = i + 1

    while i < units:
        exponent = exponent * 10 + (stringUnit(text, i) - 48)
        i = i + 1

    if negative then exponent = 0 - exponent
    exponent = exponent - fractional

    while digits != 0 && digits % 10 == 0:
        digits = digits / 10
        exponent = exponent + 1

    return Shortest {digits: digits, exponent: exponent}
